<check_type:"VMware">

<custom_item>
  type        : AUDIT_ESX
  description : "1.10 ESXI-80-000049"
  info        : "The ESXi host must uniquely identify and must authenticate organizational users by using Active Directory.

GROUP ID: V-258737
RULE ID: SV-258737r1015919

Join ESXi hosts to an Active Directory domain to eliminate the need to create and maintain multiple local user accounts. Using Active Directory for user authentication simplifies the ESXi host configuration, ensures password complexity and reuse policies are enforced, and reduces the risk of security breaches and unauthorized access.

Note: If the Active Directory group \"ESX Admins\" (default) exists, all users and groups assigned as members to this group will have full administrative access to all ESXi hosts in the domain.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000104-VMM-000500, SRG-OS-000109-VMM-000550, SRG-OS-000112-VMM-000560, SRG-OS-000113-VMM-000570, SRG-OS-000123-VMM-000620"
  solution    : "From the vSphere Client, go to Hosts and Clusters.

Select the ESXi Host >> Configure >> System >> Authentication Services.

Click \"Join Domain...\" and enter the AD domain to join.

Select the \"Using credentials\" radio button and enter the credentials of an account with permissions to join machines to AD (use UPN naming \"user@domain\"). Click \"OK\".

or

From a PowerCLI command prompt while connected to the ESXi host, run the following command:

Get-VMHost | Get-VMHostAuthentication | Set-VMHostAuthentication -JoinDomain -Domain \"domain name\" -User \"username\" -Password \"password\"

If any local user accounts are present besides root and service accounts, delete them by going to Host UI >> Manage >> Security & Users >> Users."
  reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171|3.5.1,800-171|3.5.4,800-171r3|03.01.01,800-171r3|03.05.01a.,800-171r3|03.05.04,800-53|AC-2(2),800-53|IA-2,800-53|IA-2(5),800-53|IA-2(8),800-53|IA-2(9),800-53r5|AC-2(2),800-53r5|IA-2,800-53r5|IA-2(5),800-53r5|IA-2(8),CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000764,CCI|CCI-000770,CCI|CCI-001682,CCI|CCI-001941,CCI|CCI-001942,CCI|CCI-004045,CN-L3|7.1.3.1(a),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(e),CN-L3|7.1.3.2(e),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(a),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF2.0|DE.CM-01,CSF2.0|DE.CM-03,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.2.1,ITSG-33|AC-2(2),ITSG-33|IA-2,ITSG-33|IA-2a.,ITSG-33|IA-2(5),ITSG-33|IA-2(5)(a),ITSG-33|IA-2(5)(b),ITSG-33|IA-2(8),ITSG-33|IA-2(9),LEVEL|3M,NESA|T2.3.8,NESA|T5.3.1,NESA|T5.4.2,NESA|T5.5.1,NESA|T5.5.2,NESA|T5.5.3,NIAv2|AM2,NIAv2|AM8,NIAv2|AM14b,NIAv2|AM18,NIAv2|AM28,NIAv2|NS5j,NIAv2|SS14e,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,QCSC-v1|15.2,Rule-ID|SV-258737r1015919_rule,STIG-ID|ESXI-80-000049,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.2,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-258737"
  see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23619"
  xsl_stmt    : "<xsl:template match=\"/\">"
  xsl_stmt    : "<xsl:for-each select=\"//audit:returnval\">"
  xsl_stmt    : "<xsl:value-of select=\"audit:propSet[audit:name=\'name\']/audit:val\"/> - Joined to AD Domain : <xsl:value-of select=\"audit:propSet/audit:val/audit:authenticationManagerInfo/audit:authConfig[@xsi:type='HostActiveDirectoryInfo']/audit:enabled\"/><xsl:text>&#10;</xsl:text>"
  xsl_stmt    : "</xsl:for-each>"
  xsl_stmt    : "</xsl:template>"
  regex       : "Joined to AD Domain : "
  expect      : "Joined to AD Domain : true$"
</custom_item>

<custom_item>
  type        : AUDIT_ESX
  description : "1.40 ESXI-80-000213"
  info        : "The ESXi host must disable Inter-Virtual Machine (VM) Transparent Page Sharing.

GROUP ID: V-258768
RULE ID: SV-258768r959010

Published academic papers have demonstrated that by forcing a flush and reload of cache memory, it is possible to measure memory timings to try to determine an Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) encryption key in use on another virtual machine running on the same physical processor of the host server if Transparent Page Sharing (TPS) is enabled between the two VMs. This technique works only in a highly controlled system configured in a nonstandard way that VMware believes would not be recreated in a production environment.

Although VMware believes information being disclosed in real-world conditions is unrealistic, out of an abundance of caution, upcoming ESXi update releases will no longer enable TPS between VMs by default (TPS will still be used within individual VMs)."
  solution    : "From the vSphere Client, go to Hosts and Clusters.

Select the ESXi Host >> Configure >> System >> Advanced System Settings.

Click \"Edit\". Select the \"Mem.ShareForceSalting\" value and set it to \"2\".

or

From a PowerCLI command prompt while connected to the ESXi host, run the following command:

Get-VMHost | Get-AdvancedSetting -Name Mem.ShareForceSalting | Set-AdvancedSetting -Value 2"
  reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|3M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-258768r959010_rule,STIG-ID|ESXI-80-000213,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-258768"
  see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23619"
  xsl_stmt    : "<xsl:template match=\"/\">"
  xsl_stmt    : "<xsl:for-each select=\"//audit:returnval\">"
  xsl_stmt    : "<xsl:value-of select=\"audit:propSet[audit:name='name']/audit:val\"/> - <xsl:value-of select=\"audit:propSet/audit:val/audit:option[audit:key='Mem.ShareForceSalting']/audit:key\"/> : <xsl:value-of select=\"audit:propSet/audit:val/audit:option[audit:key='Mem.ShareForceSalting']/audit:value\"/><xsl:text>&#10;</xsl:text>"
  xsl_stmt    : "</xsl:for-each>"
  xsl_stmt    : "</xsl:template>"
  regex       : "Mem\.ShareForceSalting : "
  expect      : "Mem\.ShareForceSalting : 2$"
</custom_item>

</check_type>
